103
02:24:42,404 --> 02:25:10,754
standing at the apex of Arlington and Jerome, trying to remember the black and the brown and the tan. Ronnie and Otis used to live in the big house on the corner lot. It's probably changed a half dozen times since then. Current owner's been there for a minute. He's good with his hands, knows his way around wood and tools. Place has been gussied up quite a bit. Picket fence is off-white, but if you know, you know.

104
02:25:13,893 --> 02:25:38,986
Asian kid in a Tufts hoodie just whipped by in a helmet and rollerblades. Didn't see much of that back in the day. The university sort of, sort of hid on the hillside trying its best to be a baby Ivy. But the co-eds come here all the time now. Basketballs, tennis rackets, and pickleball paddles in tow on bikes, benzos, and rollerblades.

105
02:25:39,762 --> 02:25:54,781
We used to bust ourselves up pretty good on rickety metal skates with clunky keys and leather straps. Nothing a little Vaseline and Mercurochrome couldn't handle. How did Henley put it in Invictus? Oh yeah, bloody but unbowed.

106
02:25:59,438 --> 02:26:29,240
Not too many white and off-white kids hanging out down here back then. It was as if the invisible lines drawn to keep us in sometimes kept other folks out too. Now they pretty much taken over. Duggar Park, the Rhone tennis courts, the Hervey schoolyard, and a hundred addresses on Arlington, Lincoln, and Jerome. A host of our remembered places, so few of our original faces.

107
02:26:52,072 --> 02:27:19,139
Meanwhile, back at Ronnie and Otis' old place, I'm still standing. Like that centurion, knowing that Jesus doesn't have to go in to heal his servant, he just has to speak a word. I guess I keep hoping that he'll speak a word to the cornelot, too, and bring back the black and the brown and the tan. Across the street is a river. It's low tide, but the smell is gone, and the grass is greener than I recall.

108
02:27:21,350 --> 02:27:41,380
That was our little park, away from Duggar and a lot less hectic. We had makeshift bases or discarded cones for football and softball. We lost a few in the river, but nobody was going in that muck to retrieve anything. We'd probably wade in that water today.

109
02:27:43,760 --> 02:28:05,039
They planted some trees there many years ago. They're all pretty big now. Maple and ash, I think. Nobody plays baseball or football there anymore. But there are a lot of dogs frolicking off leash. Gaggles of fat geese daring pitties and pugs and poodles to chase them off leash.

110
02:28:15,940 --> 02:28:43,463
I can't imagine my childhood without losing a few softballs there. I cannot imagine Mrs. Allen calling James Michael to come and eat, or little Charlie watching us play from his folding chair because his spindly legs were too weak to let him run. I can't imagine that I'm still here. And Ronnie and Otis, Darryl King, Frankie French, Aaron McDaniel, Maki Davis are all gone.

111
02:28:45,387 --> 02:29:09,046
Everyone had a nickname back then. Darryl was super fast, so we called him Road Runner and Jack Rabbit. Mark was as thick as a big tree trunk, so we came up with Oak for him. Aaron was Spud, Frankie was Fruit Man, and I was Top Cat. Too cool, ran to school. Everyone wants the corner lot now.

112
02:29:09,518 --> 02:29:32,941
